476" :question/id #uuid "13830fee-20a1-5f06-9a68-65b5fe7b7a28" :question/math-academy-id "q-8476" :question/problem "Consider the equation $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Given that the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(3n+1)\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-8476/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3bd5e5c-b8d1-53e5-9968-8bef8faceb4b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-8476"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-8477/selection/83ce8fa848c583fe6bae59ecdecd374997efa38abbd8bf4d91c8e120028a1f97" :answer/id #uuid "a3875b88-aa63-50a2-9a80-405381d560c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8477/selection/88fb2a80fd5647a172ff1b7743c452bc0a56c7efbb77f728b7244731af475558" :answer/id #uuid "96ff6c95-be57-5b92-85b0-41000b051c76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8477/selection/eb812f27219f6aa58813c9c74c442c8eeae741dad3c1dfa26acafe737dbee5f4" :answer/id #uuid "bd9e429a-c7ff-5b61-9512-4016137f2fd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8477/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" :answer/id #uuid "2447218f-56c9-573b-8e6a-a78d17c8c2d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8477/selection/036700265ffc6a9473c27cf0361784cfc24e841090a8239cee429a74cf087475" :answer/id #uuid "4744d360-e084-557e-9788-b79051b0a470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8477/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "72684fcf-8a1d-562b-a067-4c43bb6e8957" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-8477/selection/036700265ffc6a9473c27cf0361784cfc24e841090a8239cee429a74cf087475" :answer-field/choices ["q-8477/selection/83ce8fa848c583fe6bae59ecdecd374997efa38abbd8bf4d91c8e120028a1f97" "q-8477/selection/88fb2a80fd5647a172ff1b7743c452bc0a56c7efbb77f728b7244731af475558" "q-8477/selection/eb812f27219f6aa58813c9c74c442c8eeae741dad3c1dfa26acafe737dbee5f4" "q-8477/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" "q-8477/selection/036700265ffc6a9473c27cf0361784cfc24e841090a8239cee429a74cf087475"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-8477" :question/id #uuid "527f7f27-5579-5862-bf98-9b454d638e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-8477" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{12}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-8477/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9eb12a99-5169-52f7-8830-5de0d2d854f6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-8477"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "